@node	0.6, 0.7, 0.5, Chapter 0
@section Errors as values

This section covers the edition's error type and how this book uses it. No
exception crosses library code in this book: a fallible operation returns
or raises errors as ordinary values, and the caller decides where they
become results.

@heading @code{Raise} and @code{either}

The raising side is Arrow's @code{Raise<E>} context parameter. A function
that can fail declares the errors it produces, and @code{r.raise(e)}
aborts the nearest enclosing raise scope with the error value @code{e}.
Since @code{raise} never returns, the code after a guard needs no branch
for the failure:

@example kotlin
public sealed interface PortError {
    public data object Blank : PortError

    public data class NotDigits(
        val input: String,
    ) : PortError

    public data class OutOfRange(
        val n: Int,
    ) : PortError
}

context(r: Raise<PortError>)
public fun parsePort(s: String): Int {
    val t = s.trim()
    if (t.isEmpty()) r.raise(PortError.Blank)
    if (t.any { c -> c !in '0'..'9' }) r.raise(PortError.NotDigits(s))
    val n = t.toInt()
    if (n !in 1..65535) r.raise(PortError.OutOfRange(n))
    return n
}
@end example

@noindent
The catching side is @code{either @{ @}}, which runs its block under one
raise scope and returns an @code{Either}: @code{Either.Right} of the value
if nothing raised, @code{Either.Left} of the first error if one did. Inside
the block the context argument is supplied by the scope itself, so calls
read as plain calls:

@example kotlin
public fun parsePortEither(s: String): Either<PortError, Int> = either { parsePort(s) }

parsePortEither(" 8080 ")
// => Either.Right(8080)

parsePortEither("80x0")
// => Either.Left(NotDigits(input=80x0))
@end example

@noindent
Context parameters are a stable part of Kotlin 2.4, and this edition uses
them with no compiler flag and no opt-in annotation.

@heading Per-domain error sets

The error type is a sealed interface of the domain, one variant per failure
mode, so a caller can dispatch on errors with the exhaustive @code{when} of
@ref{0.4}. This book keeps error sets per domain: the account of
@ref{3.1} raises @code{AccountError}, the interval arithmetic of @ref{2.1.4}
raises @code{IntervalError}, and so on. One error set is shared by every
chapter that runs interpreted programs: the runtime's @code{SchemeError},
whose variants carry the book's runtime faults, from @code{Unbound} for a
name lookup that walked the whole environment chain, through
@code{TypeMismatch} and @code{DivisionByZero}, to @code{Overflow} for
checked arithmetic past the @code{Long} width. The book's own
@code{(error "message" irritant ...)} calls raise @code{UserRaised}, so
user programs of @ref{4.1} fail with values too.

@heading What this buys

Three consequences follow and are used without further comment later.
Errors compose: a helper that raises composes with any other raising
helper, and no try-and-rethrow plumbing appears. Errors stay typed all the
way to the driver: @code{Either.Left(InsufficientFunds)} is a value that
tests can compare, which is how the exercises of @ref{3.1} and @ref{4.3}
pin failure behavior. And test failure paths cost the same as success
paths: a probe asserts @code{Either.Left(NotDigits("1x"))} where an
exception-based design would assert a throw.

@example kotlin
either { parsePort("   ") }
// => Either.Left(Blank)

either { parsePort("99999") }
// => Either.Left(OutOfRange(n=99999))
@end example

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.4}Exercise 0.4:} The scaffold gives you
@code{parseAmountEager}, a parser for non-negative amounts that throws
@code{IllegalArgumentException} on blank input and on input containing any
character outside @code{0..9} after trimming spaces, alongside a sealed
@code{ParseError} with @code{Blank} and @code{NotDigits} variants. Write
@code{parseAmount} as a @code{context(r: Raise<ParseError>)} function with
the same behavior, raising instead of throwing. Test both branches through
@code{either @{ @}}: digits and surrounding spaces parse to their value,
blank input raises @code{Blank}, a stray character raises
@code{NotDigits} carrying the original input, and the eager original still
throws where your version returns a value.

